+# Local JASS/vJASS extension
+
+This checkout is CodeGraph **v1.6.2**, upstream commit
+`6560052a6f856855d3f71eee838fd66ccfa4285d`, with a local Classic Warcraft source
+extractor and resolver. It is not an official upstream release. Package version
+stays 1.6.2; the MCP/daemon identity is `1.6.2+jass-vjass.3` so a vanilla or older
+local daemon cannot silently serve this revision. Extraction version is 30;
+rebuild older indexes to include context-specific generated declarations.
+
+## What is integrated
+
+`.j` and `.jass` detect as `jass`; `.vjass` detects as `vjass`. Both dialect IDs
+use the same standalone TypeScript structural parser, inside the original
+extraction pipeline, worker pool, SQLite/FTS index, resolver and incremental sync.
+There is no subprocess bridge to jassgraph and no Go dependency. The original
+`codegraph_explore`, graph traversal, callers, callees, impact, search, CLI and
+watcher are retained. Other languages retain the original Rust/WASM paths.
+
+The extractor records functions/natives, parameters, locals/globals/constants,
+type aliases, libraries/scopes, structs, interfaces, modules, fields and methods.
+References include textual calls, explicit `function Name` callbacks, initializer
+calls, library requirements, declaration types and struct inheritance.
+Inactive `//! novjass` documentation is excluded before lexical analysis, and
+JassHelper multiline strings are masked without losing following coordinates.
+Local initializer calls belong to their enclosing function/method. Unqualified
+struct methods resolve in their owning type before same-named global functions.
+Inheritance headers exclude comments; ambiguous conditional members remain
+unresolved instead of falling through to a global homonym. Ordinary declared
+`execute`/`evaluate` methods resolve on known receivers; indirect helpers remain
+outside the supported subset. Secondary symbol/file MCP reads retain the same
+JASS coverage notes and diagnostics as exploration.
+
+Resolution is case-sensitive and restricted to JASS/vJASS declarations. It uses
+lexical scopes, private visibility, explicit public prefixes (`Library_Name`),
+declared local/parameter/field types, `this`, `thistype`, and explicit inheritance.
+Ambiguous functions and unknown method receivers remain unresolved; there is no
+fallback to a unique method elsewhere in the project. Resolved edges carry
+`metadata.jassStaticSubset` and a conservative provenance marker.
+
+## Revision 3: project projections and declared indirection
+
+The existing scoped source scanner supplies a catalog of textmacro and module
+bodies. Parameterized textmacros (quoted arguments without escapes) and nested
+module implementations are expanded as finite static projections. Each consumer
+has its own method/field declarations and thistype callback context. Module
+origin scope is retained for private library helpers; independent private module
+onInit methods have internal graph names. Cycles, ambiguity, unknown arguments
+and depth/size limits are diagnostics, never an arbitrary selected definition.
+Limits: 24 nested projections, 50,000 processed lines and 4 MiB of projected text.
+
+Projected nodes belong to the consumer file and anchor to the real invocation
+line. Their docstrings identify the original definition file/line and expansion
+chain. The shown source remains the unmodified on-disk invocation, **not** a
+verbatim expanded method body. Macro/module bodies do not become editable files.
+An edit/add/delete of a JASS source invalidates all in-scope JASS consumers for
+re-extraction, including unchanged consumers. Other languages keep their normal
+incremental path. This conservative invalidation costs more than a dependency-
+minimal refresh; it prevents stale generated members.
+
+Imports link indexed JASS source file nodes only: no external files are opened
+or indexed, and arbitrary JassHelper lookup folders are not assumed. Relative
+importer/root alternatives must identify one file. Imports generated by another
+projection are left unsupported pending compiler-order evidence.
+
+Named function/static-method execute/evaluate helpers and simple literal
+ExecuteFunc global names can resolve. Computed function names and function-
+interface values remain unresolved. Declared property getter/setters, single
+factory-return chains, and arrays with declared struct element types can resolve.
+Normal non-inheriting structs' built-in allocate/create/destroy/deallocate are
+represented as lifecycle dependencies to the type, without fabricated bodies;
+array structs get no automatic allocator. Native calls and callbacks are static
+potential dependencies, not proof of execution or callback registration firing.
+
+## Language coverage limits
+
+This is **partial static language support**, not complete JassHelper semantics:
+
+- No Zinc, hooks, delegates or keys; no general data-flow/type inference.
+- Only a simple thistype.member.exists condition backed by an unconditional
+  public declaration in that exact projected struct is simplified (including
+  its negation). Absence, compound/elseif conditions, inheritance and debug
+  flags remain unknown; ambiguous conditional definitions stay unresolved.
+- Macro-generated module catalogs, escaped macro arguments, arbitrary compiler
+  import search paths, unsupported directives and advanced preprocessor cases
+  retain explicit limits. Private member collisions across modules are not
+  fully namespaced (except module initializers); they must not be guessed.
+- Function-interface value dispatch, computed ExecuteFunc, arbitrary receiver
+  chains, index operators and full destructor/onDestroy/inheritance semantics
+  remain unsupported. Lifecycle type edges do not prove a destructor call chain.
+- Headers are line-based. Invalid constructs and unsupported multiline headers
+  report diagnostics; this is not a compiler. Coordinates use one-based lines
+  and UTF-16 columns; sources follow the existing UTF-8 pipeline.
+- Missing edges do not establish missing consumers; index state complete only
+  describes the indexing operation. A clean graph is not a validation of the map.
+
+MCP responses retain coverage notes and relevant diagnostics. JassHelper/pJASS
+with the actual Classic APIs remain the compilation validators; game and
+multiplayer behavior require their own tests.
+
+## Local operation
+
+The Linux launcher is `/home/snz/.local/bin/codegraph`. It runs the Node 24.16.0
+runtime shipped in the verified upstream linux-x64 package, isolated under
+`/home/snz/.local/share/codegraph-vjass/runtime/`; it does not change CachyOS's
+system Node 26. Upstream's Linux kernel binary is retained for its supported
+languages. The launcher disables telemetry and update checks; avoid `upgrade`,
+which would replace local language changes with an upstream build.
+
+```bash
+codegraph init /absolute/source/project --yes
+codegraph query Ping --path /absolute/source/project --json
+codegraph explore 'Init Ping' --path /absolute/source/project
+codegraph sync /absolute/source/project
+```
+
+Create an index only in the project selected for the task; this writes a
+`.codegraph/` index. Sources and map archives are not rewritten. Old indexes
+should be rebuilt with `codegraph index /absolute/source/project`.
+MCP registration uses the original `serve --mcp`; default upstream tool discovery
+exposes `codegraph_explore`. The other original tools remain in the CLI/API and
+can be explicitly enabled with `CODEGRAPH_MCP_TOOLS=explore,search,node,callers,callees,impact,files,status`.
+
+## Evidence and reproducibility
+
+`__tests__/jass-vjass.test.ts` exercises the real extractor and SQLite pipeline,
+including private/public scopes, callback references, declared method receivers,
+ambiguity, persistent reopen and incremental/fresh-rebuild edge-set convergence.
+`scripts/probe-jass.mjs ROOT...` runs three static flow probes per selected root,
+then reconstructs the indexes and checks counts and unchanged source hashes.
